###### Abstract

The flat-spectrum radio quasar 3C454.3 underwent an extraordinary 5-day \\gamma-ray outburst in November 2010 when the daily flux measured with the Fermi Large Area Telescope (LAT) at photon energies E>100\\,MeV reached (66\\pm 2)\\times 10^{-6}ph cm{}^{-2}s{}^{-1}. This is a factor of 3 higher than its previous maximum flux recorded in December 2009 and \\gtrsim 5 times brighter than the Vela pulsar, which is normally the brightest source in the \\gamma-ray sky. The 3-hr peak flux was (85\\pm5)\\times 10^{-6}ph cm{}^{-2}s{}^{-1}, corresponding to an apparent isotropic luminosity of (2.1\\pm0.2)\\times 10^{50} erg s{}^{-1}, the highest ever recorded for a blazar. In this paper, we investigate the features of this exceptional event in the \\gamma-ray band of the Fermi-LAT. In contrast to previous flares of the same source observed with the Fermi-LAT, clear spectral changes are observed during the flare.

